{"authored" [{"question" "q-161496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nUse $[a,b]$ as the full nonempty projection of the exactly specified region $R$ onto the $y$ axis. The inner limits may be piecewise. What is $a+b?$" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/review.json" "file_sha256" "7e807ac39cc7eaaae4b08f32468c5c30ff55c7a01a8c8350f84ca141cf2f2b5f" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "5ffee70bdf8bbfdd6867ab481e5633b189cda60d20c6512b9202bdbda11eb762" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/verification.json" "basis" 2020 "rationale" "Retain original R exactly, require its full actual nonempty projection, enforce ordered fibers, and distinguish source printed interval/accepted key from study mathematics. q161496 projection [1,8/3], answer11/3; q161495 projection[-2,4], answer2 with cap split at-1."} {"question" "q-161496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The inequalities defining $R$ are retained exactly. A fiber is nonempty only when $x\\le4-y$. Combining this with $x\\ge y/2$ gives $y/2\\le4-y$, hence $y\\le8/3$. Every $y\\in[1,8/3]$ is attained, for example at $x=y/2$, $z=0$. Therefore the full nonempty projection is $[a,b]=[1,8/3]$ and\n\n$$a+b=1+\\frac83=\\frac{11}{3}.$$\n\nThe actual base is $D=\\{(x,y):1\\le y\\le8/3,\\ y/2\\le x\\le\\min(2,4-y)\\}$. The upper $x$ bounds meet at $y=2$, so the triple integral is\n\n$$\\iiint_R f\\,dV=\\int_1^2\\int_{y/2}^2\\int_0^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy+\\int_2^{8/3}\\int_{y/2}^{4-y}\\int_0^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.$$\n\nFor $f=1$, these terms are $35/24$ and $1/9$, giving volume $113/72$. Using the printed nominal limits $1\\le y\\le4$ and $y/2\\le x\\le2$ instead includes reversed $z$ bounds. That formal signed integral gives $9/8$ and does not integrate over $R$. The source accepted the printed-endpoint sum $5$; the corrected study question asks for the full actual projection and adds $11/3$ as the correct choice." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/review.json" "file_sha256" "7e807ac39cc7eaaae4b08f32468c5c30ff55c7a01a8c8350f84ca141cf2f2b5f" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "5ffee70bdf8bbfdd6867ab481e5633b189cda60d20c6512b9202bdbda11eb762" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/verification.json" "basis" 2020 "rationale" "Retain original R exactly, require its full actual nonempty projection, enforce ordered fibers, and distinguish source printed interval/accepted key from study mathematics. q161496 projection [1,8/3], answer11/3; q161495 projection[-2,4], answer2 with cap split at-1."} {"question" "q-161496" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{11}{3}"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/review.json" "file_sha256" "7e807ac39cc7eaaae4b08f32468c5c30ff55c7a01a8c8350f84ca141cf2f2b5f" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "5ffee70bdf8bbfdd6867ab481e5633b189cda60d20c6512b9202bdbda11eb762" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-161496/verification.json" "basis" 2020 "rationale" "Retain original R exactly, require its full actual nonempty projection, enforce ordered fibers, and distinguish source printed interval/accepted key from study mathematics. q161496 projection [1,8/3], answer11/3; q161495 projection[-2,4], answer2 with cap split at-1."} {"question" "q-161496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "The triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={\\int }_{a}^{b}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}{\\int }_{∗}^{∗}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy\n$$\n\nis defined over the type I region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4,\\,0\\le z\\le 4-x-y}.\n$$\n\nWhat is $a+b?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a0c8097a784b55c27406d8ffdb95f891f6b2c43f5ad3aff3c0357fb22ab15f0" "basis" 2019 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A type I region in three-dimensional space can be written as\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,(x,y)\\in D,\\,{u}_{1}(x,y)\\le z\\le {u}_{2}(x,y)}.\n$$\n\nA typical three-dimensional type I region can be visualized schematically, as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/assets/1bd4b6d0b1aa955921c1f7855d3fbd95417072a67504d9de9be44dfbbed592e5.png)\n\n \n\nIf $R$ is a type I region, we can express the triple integral of $f(x,y,z)$ over $R$ as a mixed integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{{u}_{1}(x,y)}^{{u}_{2}(x,y)}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}z]\\,\\text{d}A\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\n{u}_{1}(x,y)=0,\\,{u}_{2}(x,y)=4-x-y.\n$$\n\nTherefore, our triple integral can be written as\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\,\\text{d}V={∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA.\n$$\n\nNow, the region $D$ is given by\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}y\\le x\\le 2,\\,1\\le y\\le 4}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by expressing the double integral over $D$ as a repeated integral, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dA & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2}[{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz]dx\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}{\\int }_{y/2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{4-x-y}f(x,y,z)\\,dz\\,dx\\,dy.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\na+b=1+4=5.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/state.json" "file_sha256" "5a0c8097a784b55c27406d8ffdb95f891f6b2c43f5ad3aff3c0357fb22ab15f0" "basis" 2019 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-161496" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "986d45b074d202621525e2f4d579743e1ed7e1f54a93934fb99f31c5b0e63474" "basis" 2019 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14062541/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [] "usage" {"q-161496" []} "basis" 2023 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [17592186344238 17592186344239 17592186344240 17592186344241 17592186344242 17592186344279] "immutable_fields" [[17592186344278 :answer-field/correct] [17592186344278 :answer-field/id] [17592186344278 :answer-field/key] [17592186344278 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
